{#
  ★★ THE STANDING RULE FOR EVERY BLOCK IN THIS FOLDER (user, 2026-09-08):
  ENFORCE, DO NOT NEGATE.

      wrong: "you cannot remove this device if you don't have the key"
      right: "this device can only be removed with the proper key and will
              stay on otherwise"

  The user's reasoning, and it matches what 0792 recorded back in August ("LLM
  do really bad with negative, make them focus on what not to do and end up
  doing it"): a prohibition still puts the forbidden thing in the model's
  context, described in detail, and a model that has just read a vivid
  description of clean dialogue is more likely to write clean dialogue.

  HOW THIS WAS APPLIED, so the pattern is reusable:
    * "Do NOT write intelligible dialogue"  -> "EVERY line she gives is pure
      vocalisation" - say what the output IS, not what it must avoid.
    * "Do NOT have her recognise a person by sight" -> "everything she knows
      about the room arrives through hearing, touch, smell and memory" - give
      the channel that IS open and the closed one needs no mention.
    * "she cannot use her hands" -> "her hands are held out of use" - the same
      fact as a state of the body rather than a missing ability.
    * "It does not come off on its own" -> "It stays exactly as it was put on
      ... time is on the device's side."

  ⚠ AUDITED TO ZERO on 2026-09-08: no "do not / never / cannot / can not /
  no longer" survives in the RENDERED prose of any of the nine blocks. Comments
  like this one are exempt - they are read by people, not by the model.
  Re-run the check before shipping a prompt edit; it is trivial to regress.
#}
{#
  VRTE DD/ZaZ AddOn - the restraints came OFF.  (2026-08-24, positive rewrite)

  The third release block, covering all three ACTION categories at once (arms,
  legs, all) because they share one instruction: you may act again. The gag and
  the blindfold get their own files because what they restore differs in kind -
  speech and sight.

  User's rule, applied to every category rather than just the gag: "make sure
  each of those restraint have a 3x prompt recovery, like the gag so the LLM
  know that this NPC is not gag/blind/restraint anymore".

  ⚠⚠ WRITTEN IN THE POSITIVE (user, 2026-08-24): "LLM do really bad with
  negative, make them focus on what not to do and end up doing it." The old
  wording was a list of things to stop doing - "do not keep refusing on physical
  grounds, do not keep having her ask someone else" - which put refusing and
  asking-someone-else in front of the model at the exact moment it needed to
  forget them. It now names the capability instead, and gives it something to
  DO with it: the ACTION line is offered rather than un-forbidden.

  Each decorator returns "3"/"2"/"1" for the next three renders after that
  category ends, then "". Each returns "" while its restraint is STILL on, so a
  release block can never sit alongside its own constraint block, and re-binding
  cancels the countdown on the very next render.

  ⚠ Guarded with isString, never `!= ""` - see 0910.
  ⚠ One header, not three: freeing arms and legs in the same moment should read
    as one event, which is what happens when a full-body restraint comes off.
#}
{% if exists("npc") and existsIn(npc, "UUID") %}
{# ★ 1.3.0 (2026-09-14): STATE FILE FIRST, decorator as the fallback. VRTE_DDZaZ.dll writes
   Data/SKSE/Plugins/VRTE_DDZaZ/prompt_state.json the instant a device goes on or off, a name
   arrives, a restraint is released; read_json re-reads on the file's mtime and the random second
   argument defeats SkyrimNet's 55 s callback cache (VRTouchEvents' recipe, VR-verified, report 45).
   Matched on the SkyrimNet entity UUID. No entry for her = the old decorator path, unchanged. #}
{% set _ds = read_json("VRTE_DDZaZ/prompt_state", random * 10000 + random * 100 + random) %}
{% set _de = 0 %}
{% if isObject(_ds) and existsIn(_ds, "npcs") %}{% for _e in _ds.npcs %}{% if _e.uuid == npc.UUID %}{% set _de = _e %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}{% endif %}
{% if isObject(_de) and existsIn(_de, "unbound_arms") %}{% set _vd_ua = _de.unbound_arms %}{% else %}{% set _vd_ua = vrtedd_unbound_arms(npc.UUID) %}{% endif %}
{% if isObject(_de) and existsIn(_de, "unbound_legs") %}{% set _vd_ul = _de.unbound_legs %}{% else %}{% set _vd_ul = vrtedd_unbound_legs(npc.UUID) %}{% endif %}
{% if isObject(_de) and existsIn(_de, "unbound_all") %}{% set _vd_uall = _de.unbound_all %}{% else %}{% set _vd_uall = vrtedd_unbound_all(npc.UUID) %}{% endif %}
{% set _vd_ua_on = (isString(_vd_ua) and _vd_ua != "") %}
{% set _vd_ul_on = (isString(_vd_ul) and _vd_ul != "") %}
{% set _vd_uall_on = (isString(_vd_uall) and _vd_uall != "") %}
{% if _vd_ua_on or _vd_ul_on or _vd_uall_on %}
{% set _p = "he" %}{% set _pp = "his" %}{% set _po = "him" %}
{% if decnpc(npc.UUID).isFemale %}{% set _p = "she" %}{% set _pp = "her" %}{% set _po = "her" %}{% endif %}
**THE RESTRAINTS ARE OFF — {{ decnpc(npc.UUID).name }} CAN ACT AGAIN.**
{% if _vd_uall_on %}
{{ _p | capitalize }} has just been freed completely. {{ _pp | capitalize }} hands and {{ _pp }} legs are {{ _pp }} own again, and {{ _p }} can do anything {{ _p }} could do before it went on.
{% endif %}
{% if _vd_ua_on %}
{{ _pp | capitalize }} arms have just been freed. {{ _p | capitalize }} reaches, takes, holds, hands things over, works a latch, and draws a weapon.
{% endif %}
{% if _vd_ul_on %}
{{ _pp | capitalize }} legs have just been freed. {{ _p | capitalize }} walks and runs at {{ _pp }} own pace.
{% endif %}
**Write {{ _po }} as someone with {{ _pp }} body back.** Where the turns above have {{ _po }} unable to do a thing, this reply starts from being able to. If {{ _p }} agreed to something and could not carry it out, {{ _p }} can carry it out now — and if an `ACTION:` line fits what {{ _p }} says, use it.

Physical acts that were impossible a moment ago are available again — reaching, lifting, carrying, opening a door, walking away. Which of them {{ _p }} uses, if any, follows from what {{ _p }} says and from who {{ _p }} is.

{{ _p | capitalize }} may roll {{ _pp }} shoulders or shake out {{ _pp }} legs as {{ _p }} starts. Any ache or weakness the restraint left is told in the block about what it left behind, and that decides how smoothly each of these goes.

How {{ _p }} feels about having been restrained, or about being free, is yours to play.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
